 ## Overview
-Detects if a ray intersects a zero-crossing (surface) at the voxel level of a grid in the accessor. It performs empty-space ray-marching at all levels of the tree using an HDDA (Hierarchical Distance Field Acceleration). It is optimized for performance, precision, and correctness, and surfaces a shared cache-hint helper for other operations. It is SDF-sign-agnostic and reports the EXIT crossing for rays that start inside the surface, matching nanovdb::ZeroCrossing behavior.
+Detects if a ray intersects a zero-crossing at the voxel level of a grid in the accessor [S1]. It performs empty-space ray-marching at all levels of the tree using an HDDA (Hierarchical Distance Field Acceleration) [S1]. The method is optimized for performance, precision, and correctness, and surfaces a shared cache-hint helper for other operations [S2, unofficial]. It is SDF-sign-agnostic and reports the EXIT crossing for rays that start inside the surface, matching nanovdb::ZeroCrossing behavior [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   `ray`: Ray object with origin $\mathbf{o}$, direction $\mathbf{d}$, and methods `t0()`, `t1()`, `invDir()`, `operator()(time)`, and `clip(bbox)`.
-*   `acc`: Accessor providing `getValue(ijk)`, `isActive(ijk)`, `getDim(ijk, ray)`, and `root().bbox()`.
-*   `ijk`: Output coordinate of the closest voxel after the intersection point.
-*   `v`: Output grid value at the intersection coordinate `ijk`.
-*   `t`: Output time of the intersection along the ray.
